1 to 2\ninches of new rainfall for portions of southeast UT and southwest CO.\n\nThis is a preliminary forecast that hinges on slight shifts in a\nstrong moisture plume impacting the region. It also attempts to\ncapture the most statistically likely outcome, despite stronger and\nweaker outlier predictions. Outdoor enthusiasts, motorists, and\nresidents of recent flood impacted areas are encouraged to remain\nabreast of the developing forecast in the coming days.","ends":"2026-10-30T15:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; San Miguel, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.8fb5d34f2d13ed8ac1ea289950d1e46b096a7bdd.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.8fb5d34f2d13ed8ac1ea289950d1e46b096a7bdd.001.1"},{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued October 28 at 2:42PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nLow pressure begins to dig southward from the PACNW on Friday,\ntapping into a fetch of Pacific moisture that will evolve into an\natmospheric river streaming into the Desert Southwest this weekend.\nShowers and thunderstorms develop on Friday and Saturday across the\nregion. Lighter rainfall totals Friday and Saturday will reflect,\ninitially lower, moisture. However, a few thunderstorms could produce\nimpactful downpours on saturated soils left over from last weekend's\nhistoric rainfall.\n\nDeeper moisture develops Saturday night and early Sunday. This is\nexpected to produce heavier and more widespread rainfall across\nsoutheast UT and southwest CO Sunday and Monday, mostly below 10000\nfeet. While rivers are forecast to remain below action stage, a\ngrowing concern lies in the potential for runoff around saturated\nhillsides in the region. This could produce rock, mud, and debris\nflows onto roadways, as well as flash flood prone burn scars, washes,\nslot canyons, and arroyos. Early estimates predict an area of 1 to 2\ninches of new rainfall for portions of southeast UT and southwest CO.\n\nThis is a preliminary forecast that hinges on slight shifts in a\nstrong moisture plume impacting the region. It also attempts to\ncapture the most statistically likely outcome, despite stronger and\nweaker outlier predictions. Outdoor enthusiasts, motorists, and\nresidents of recent flood impacted areas are encouraged to remain\nabreast of the developing forecast in the coming days.","ends":"2026-10-29T15:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; San Miguel, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.38c97534038bb54c112c5ff0215b743f983d5cfb.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.38c97534038bb54c112c5ff0215b743f983d5cfb.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-29T17:13:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 291713 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 1113 AM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Mild fall conditions continue today. - Precipitation chances return Friday with light rain at the lower elevations and flurries above 10,000 ft. - Deeper moisture arrives Saturday night and will increase the chances for impactful precipitation for southwe","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-29T06:35:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... This is an automatically generated forecast with no discussion. The normal Fire Weather Forecast will resume in its entirety in the spring. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN BE EXPECTED NEAR ANY SHOWERS OR THUNDERSTORMS... ...THE SMOKE DATA IS CALCULATED FROM AN AVERAGE AT 6000 FEET MSL... COZ200-292200- Little Snake Forecast Area- 1235 AM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"nwd01","station_name":"Norwood","distance_km":2.3,"soil_temp_5cm